d ${x}_{4}=-4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =1(x-3)(x-3)(x-3)(x-(-4)) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x-3)(x-3)(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)(x-3)(x-3)(x+4) & =({x}^{2}-6x+9)({x}^{2}+x-12) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+72x+9{x}^{2}+9x-108 \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+81x-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+81x-108.$" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364773 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364774 13194139535750 true] [17592186364773 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364775 13194139535750 true] [17592186364773 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535750 true] [17592186364773 1041 "field-1" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364773 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364774 13194139535750 true] [17592186364773 1110 #uuid "47bddf47-4766-57e7-a6c8-43b860bb9e1a" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364774 1034 "x^4-5x^3-9x^2+81x" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364774 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535750 true] [17592186364774 1111 #uuid "25a15d76-4e4e-599a-b666-f0d8aaa86456" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364775 1034 "x^4-5x^3+9x^2+81x" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364775 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535750 true] [17592186364775 1111 #uuid "9333a3cf-f9dc-51f0-be60-a22d5bb41b15" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364776 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364777 13194139535750 true] [17592186364776 1089 "q-320185" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364776 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535750 true] [17592186364776 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070073/assets/6a39af16798593716291ab84bfff4460279420f493bd10959782312187f0bf44.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,72$" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364776 1113 #uuid "497c280e-c744-5ef7-8ff0-f0aa5098f06b" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364776 1145 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=\\pm \\text{i}$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(x-(-\\text{i}))(x-\\text{i})(x-(-6\\text{i}))(x-6\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2(x+\\text{i})(x-\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i})(x-6\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+\\text{i})(x-\\text{i})(x+6\\text{i})(x-6\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+(\\text{i}-\\text{i})x-{\\text{i}}^{2})({x}^{2}+(6\\text{i}-6\\text{i})x-36{\\text{i}}^{2}) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+1)({x}^{2}+36) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}+36{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+36) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}+37{x}^{2}+36) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+74{x}^{2}+72 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+74{x}^{2}+72.$" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364777 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364778 13194139535750 true] [17592186364777 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535750 true] [17592186364777 1041 "field-1" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364777 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364778 13194139535750 true] [17592186364777 1110 #uuid "2cb35c7d-18c8-52f7-ad41-3fcf23b6b6af" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364778 1034 "2x^4+74x^2" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364778 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535750 true] [17592186364778 1111 #uuid "5c7cad9f-bea4-593e-97b7-4e71ed4be833" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364779 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364780 13194139535750 true] [17592186364779 1089 "q-320235" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364779 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535750 true] [17592186364779 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070073/assets/4f960f00fbcbde79dee3603112ed08e8c34cf87b3ccbe781ee5acbf61a4482fd.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Determine the **expanded form** of $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $2.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,52$" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364779 1113 #uuid "dd0f01ce-b194-5637-b280-44af6aaf3b75" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364779 1145 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and ${x}_{3,4}=1\\pm \\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=2.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(x-(-2+3\\text{i}))(x-(-2-3\\text{i}))(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-(-2+3\\text{i}))(x-(-2-3\\text{i}))(x-(1+\\text{i}))(x-(1-\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+4x+4+9)({x}^{2}-2x+1+1) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+4x+13)({x}^{2}-2x+2) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+8x+13{x}^{2}-26x+26) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-18x+26) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-36x+52 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-36x+52.$" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364781 13194139535750 true] [17592186364780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364782 13194139535750 true] [17592186364780 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535750 true] [17592186364780 1041 "field-1" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364780 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364782 13194139535750 true] [17592186364780 1110 #uuid "d3bba291-4c9a-5b75-9888-645ba747b77a" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364781 1034 "2x^4+4x^3+14x^2+36x" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364781 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535750 true] [17592186364781 1111 #uuid "ab1d19fe-4d08-593d-ab6f-1d84988a44ad" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364782 1034 "2x^4+4x^3+14x^2-36x" 13194139535750 true] [17592186364782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535750 true] [17592186364782 1111 #uuid "24862d33-25bb-5eae